#
# CORRECTION, written after the candT25 bench row contradicted the first
# reading of this log. The >23 min stage is NOT prover work.
#
# Between `committed` and `statement built`, pin_instance calls dense_eval --
# the TEST's own reference evaluation of the message polynomial at the drawn
# point, needed to make the statement a true one. It is
# blocks * rows * popcount(j) ring multiplications: 1024 * 1024 * ~5 = 5.2
# MILLION Rq::mul calls, and that is where the time went. It is scaffolding, and
# no amount of prover optimization touches it.
#
# `carrier_from_raw` at 1024 blocks is about 165 s, not 23 minutes: the
# `quadeval/carrier_from_raw/8` row reads 1.286 s at eight blocks, and the
# function is linear in blocks.
#
# What survives of the finding, and it is still worth acting on:
# carrier_from_raw dots the SAME `a` against every block, so `dot_fused`
# re-transforms all 1024 entries of `a` on each of 1024 blocks -- 1023/1024 of
# that work redundant. At ~165 s against the commitment's 546 s that is 23% of
# the pre-round prover, and preparing `a` once should take -46% of it. Candidate
# T25. The justification is the bench row, not this log.
#
# LESSON: a profile stage is only as attributable as the code between its two
# prints. This one bundles prover work with test scaffolding, and the first
# reading blamed the prover for 23 minutes of `dense_eval`. The bench row is
# what caught it, which is exactly why the accept rule insists on one.
